        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(agents): guard NaN timestamp in compaction boundary check",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nIn `src/agents/sessions/agent-session.ts`, the compaction boundary timestamp check uses `new Date(compactionEntry.timestamp).getTime()` without a `Number.isNaN()` guard. If the timestamp string is unparseable, `NaN` is returned, and the comparison `<= NaN` always evaluates to `false` in JavaScript.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe `assistantIsFromBeforeCompaction` flag never evaluates to `true` when the timestamp is NaN. This means stale pre-compaction assistant messages are NOT detected, which can:\n1. Trigger unnecessary duplicate compaction cycles (extra LLM API calls)\n2. Cause OOM from runaway context growth when compaction silently fails\n\nThe same issue affects the usage-message staleness check at line 2089.\n\n## User Impact\n\nFixes a subtle correctness bug in auto-compaction that could cause duplicate LLM calls and context growth in edge cases with malformed timestamps.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Source inspection of `src/agents/sessions/agent-session.ts` lines ~2042 and ~2089\n- Unit tests: `compaction-partial-summary.test.ts` — 9 passed\n- Build: `pnpm build` succeeds\n- Runtime: `pnpm openclaw --version` / `pnpm openclaw doctor` — no regressions\n\nFixes #99444\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
